    podstata:
      "Your engine is freedom. You cannot bear being told that this is how it is done because this is how it has always been done. You try new methods, you watch what people do elsewhere and in other sports, and you are not afraid to abandon a procedure that suits everybody else. Because of that you often find the thing that fits you better than the universal plan, and usually a year before it turns into a trend. You are at your strongest in the periods of searching: after an injury, after a change of category, when a new technique is being built. The risk is the same as the strength. When freedom loses its direction, it turns into an inability to stay with anything. A new method is always more tempting than finishing off the current one, and so you change training plans, coaches and environments before they have had time to take effect. And yet in sport almost everything only breaks through at the point where you have repeated it, boringly, for a long time. Your career grows when you set aside room for searching and let the rest run in routine, even when it bores you.",
    vPodnikani:
      "In training you are curious and self-directed: you keep an eye on your data, you read, you try additions to your preparation that nobody around you is doing. When you are given a plan without an explanation, you do it worse than a plan you understand. In competition you are fired up by an unfamiliar setting, a new opponent, unusual conditions; the seventh start in the same arena, on the other hand, is harder for you than for anyone else. Long, monotonous blocks of preparation are your weak point, and they are exactly what makes you better. You work best when the routine has a clearly set end and when you know what it leads to.",
