    dar: "fighting spirit, discipline and the courage to carry responsibility for the result",
    podstata:
      "You need an opponent, a bar to clear and a clear result. An obstacle wakes you up, pressure sharpens you, and at the moment when it is decided you want to be there: the last shot, the last lap, the decisive attempt. Where other people's bodies seize up, your head clears, because at last something is at stake. Discipline is no problem for you, because you can see what it leads to, and hard graft without an opponent bores you. This is the archetype we value most in sport, and also the one most often undone by its own strengths. Because when a race is missing, you manufacture one: out of a team-mate, out of the coach, out of yourself. You read rest as weakness, tiredness as an excuse and pain as something you simply walk through. That is exactly the road to overtraining, to injury and to having nothing left to draw on at the moment when it really matters. The best version of this type is as hard about recovery as about training, because they have grasped that rest is part of the fight too.",
    vPodnikani:
      "In training you pull the whole group along: you lift the pace, you refuse to lose even a practice game, and when the group is lazy you needle it. In competition you grow in proportion to what is at stake; small starts do not interest you and you go through the motions in them. Your numbers tend to be best in the hardest period, because under pressure your head clears. The weak point is monotonous work with no measurable goal, and the phase where you are meant to ease off deliberately: you take that as a retreat, not as preparation.",
