      "Your energy comes from disagreement. You can see what has gone rigid in your sport: drills that are done out of habit, authorities who have achieved nothing, rules that protect the system rather than the athlete. And you cannot stay quiet about it. Your best performances come when somebody tells you it cannot be done, or when they write you off. Defiance is a fuel others do not have, and that is exactly why you pull off things that on paper cannot be done. The risk is obvious: defiance without direction is merely conflict. When a real opponent is missing, you start fighting your own team, your coach and in the end yourself. You tear down methods before you have a replacement, and you change environments whenever somebody restricts you. The best version of this type chooses where to fight: outwards against what genuinely does not work, while inwardly holding discipline, because without it an Outlaw turns into nothing more than a dissatisfied athlete.",
    vPodnikani:
      "In training you are the athlete others find uncomfortable, and you are direct: you ask why something is being done, and when you get no answer you do it your own way. A standard plan suits you worse than one tailored to you, even if the tailored one is objectively poorer. In competition you are most dangerous in the role of the outsider nobody believes in, and conversely you cannot settle into the role of the favourite, where all that is left is to confirm expectations. Referees' decisions and unfairness can derail you more than tiredness does.",
